david our state continues to seek  volunteers with a background  in the medical or science fields to  assist in the pandemic  and as the i-team's vanessa murphy shows  us staffing  is an issue inside hospitals  some hospital reps tell me health care  workers are doing  overtime and hospitals are actively  trying to find  more workers there are several issues  here this is a nationwide surge of  covet-19  cases there's a nationwide shortage of  health care workers  and nevada has to compete for those  workers i reached out to  hospitals in southern nevada a  spokeswoman for mountain view hospital  says they're doing everything they can  to recruit new nurses and clinical staff  they're sourcing additional nurses  through external  agencies current ratios are within  guidelines  and bonuses are offered to staff in high  need areas for  extra shifts worked as well as sign-on  bonuses  a spokesman for umc says they're well  staffed thanks to nurses and  other staff working overtime and a  spokesman for dignity health says  hospitals are constantly adjusting staff  and they've gone to great lengths to  hire more staffing including  traveling nurses to care for a surge in  patients  the nevada hospital association does not  provide numbers for specific  hospitals we've asked to find out just  how severe  any staffing shortages may be the  association releases general numbers by  county  so for example right now it reports that  81 percent of staffed hospital beds in  clark county  are full as i mentioned there's a big  need for nurses  and also lab techs to help process the  covid19  tests which takes training and  certification  vanessa murphy 8 news now
 

